Information Age - Technology Inventory
Law, Justice & Contract

Smart Contracts: Self-executing contracts with the terms directly written into code.
Digital Rights Management (DRM): Access control technologies for restricting the use of proprietary hardware and copyrighted works.
Digital Forensics: The recovery and investigation of material found in digital devices.

Governance, Administration & Institutions

Biometric Identification Systems: Automated recognition of individuals based on biological and behavioral characteristics (fingerprint, iris, facial recognition).
Electronic Voting Systems: Technologies allowing for the casting and counting of votes digitally.
Public Key Infrastructure (PKI): The framework of encryption and cybersecurity that enables secure digital identity and communication.
RFID Technology: Radio-frequency identification for tracking and access control.

Trade, Markets & Finance

High-Frequency Trading (HFT): Algorithms enabling transaction execution at speeds measuring in microseconds.
Distributed Ledger Technology (Blockchain): Decentralized digital recording systems.
Electronic Funds Transfer (EFT): The digital transfer of money (credit cards, SWIFT).
E-Commerce Platforms: Integrated software systems handling online sales, logistics, and payments.
FinTech & Mobile Payments: NFC (Near Field Communication) and app-based financial transaction systems.
Automated Clearing House (ACH): Batch electronic payment system.
Cryptocurrency: Decentralized digital currencies (Bitcoin, etc.).

Science, Education & Inquiry

Next-Generation Sequencing (NGS): High-throughput DNA sequencing technologies.
Atomic Force Microscopy: Imaging at the resolution of fractions of a nanometer.
Computational Fluid Dynamics (CFD): Using numerical analysis to solve problems that involve fluid flows.
Citizen Science Platforms: Digital networks allowing public participation in data collection and analysis.
CRISPR-Cas9 Gene Editing: Revolutionary precision gene editing technology.
Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR): DNA amplification technology.
Bioinformatics/Computational Biology: Computational analysis of biological data.

Mathematics & Quantification

Public-Key Cryptography (RSA/Elliptic Curve): Mathematical schemes that allow secure communication without pre-shared keys.
Finite Element Analysis (FEA): Computerized method for predicting how a product reacts to real-world forces.
Monte Carlo Simulations: Computational algorithms that rely on repeated random sampling to obtain numerical results.

Timekeeping, Weights & Measures

Cesium Atomic Clock: The primary standard for timekeeping and frequency in the Information Age.
Network Time Protocol (NTP): Algorithms for synchronizing clocks of computer systems over packet-switched, variable-latency data networks.

Cartography & Geographic Knowledge

Geographic Information Systems (GIS): Frameworks for gathering, managing, and analyzing spatial and geographic data.
Satellite Remote Sensing: Scanning of the earth by satellite (e.g., Landsat, SAR).

Writing & Notation Systems

Unicode Standard: A character coding system designed to support the worldwide interchange, processing, and display of the written texts.
Optical Character Recognition (OCR): Electronic conversion of images of typed, handwritten, or printed text into machine-encoded text.
QR Codes: Two-dimensional matrix barcodes.

Texts, Print & Archives

Relational Database Management Systems (RDBMS): The structural backbone of organized digital data (SQL).
Search Engines: Software systems designed to carry out web searches.
Version Control Systems: Software tools for tracking changes to source code (e.g., Git).
Digital Libraries & Repositories: Mass digitization and storage of texts (e.g., JSTOR, Google Books).
World Wide Web (HTTP/HTML): The hypertext system that made the internet accessible.
Email Systems (SMTP): Asynchronous digital communication protocol.

Communication & Telecommunications

Fiber Optic Transmission: Data transmission via light pulses through glass fibers.
Cellular Network Standards: The progression of mobile voice and data (GSM, LTE, 5G).
Wi-Fi (IEEE 802.11): Wireless local area networking.
Communication Satellites: Geostationary and Low Earth Orbit (LEO) relays.
Bluetooth: Short-range wireless technology standard.
Internet Protocol Suite (TCP/IP): The fundamental protocols enabling the internet.
Undersea Fiber Optic Cables: The physical backbone of global internet connectivity.

Algorithms, Data & AI

Operating Systems (Kernel & Shell): The fundamental software managing hardware resources (Unix/Linux, Windows).
Compilers & Interpreters: Translating high-level programming code into machine language.
Graphical User Interface (GUI): Visual ways for humans to interact with electronic devices.
Computer Vision: Algorithms allowing computers to derive meaningful information from digital images.
Cybersecurity Suites: Firewalls, VPNs, and antivirus heuristics.
Microprocessors/Integrated Circuits: The foundational technology of all computing.
Machine Learning/Deep Learning: Neural networks and artificial intelligence systems.
Cloud Computing Infrastructure: Distributed computing and data centers.
Natural Language Processing: Enabling human-computer interaction through language.

Music, Rhythm & Dance

Digital Audio Workstations (DAW): Electronic systems for recording, editing, and producing audio.
Digital Synthesizers: Sound synthesis using digital signal processing (FM synthesis, Wavetable).
Audio Compression Codecs: Algorithms reducing file size for transmission (MP3, AAC).
Streaming Media Protocols: Digital content delivery systems (RTSP, HLS).

Visual Art & Iconography

Computer-Generated Imagery (CGI): Creating still or animated visual content with computer software.
3D Modeling & Rendering: Mathematical representation of 3-dimensional surfaces.
Digital Photography (CCD/CMOS Sensors): Capturing images via electronic photodetectors.
OLED/LED Displays: Organic light-emitting diodes and LED display technology.
Virtual Reality/Augmented Reality: Immersive digital environments and overlays.
Digital Video Codecs: Compression algorithms for moving images (H.264, MPEG).

Plant Domestication & Crop Diversity

Genetically Modified Organisms (Transgenics): Direct manipulation of an organism's genes using biotechnology.

Agriculture & Field Systems

Precision Agriculture: Using IoT sensors, GPS, and big data to optimize crop yields.
Hydroponics & Vertical Farming: Growing plants without soil, often in controlled indoor environments.

Food Processing, Preservation & Bioprocessing

Industrial Fermentation: Large-scale cultivation of microbes for pharmaceuticals or food ingredients.
Cold Chain Logistics: Temperature-controlled supply chains.

Textiles & Clothing

Synthetic Microfibers: Ultra-fine synthetic fibers (e.g., polyester fleece).
Performance Membranes: Waterproof/breathable fabrics (e.g., Gore-Tex).

Glass & Optics

Liquid Crystal Displays (LCD): Flat-panel displays using light-modulating properties of liquid crystals.
Gorilla Glass (Aluminosilicate): Chemically strengthened glass used in mobile devices.
Lasers: Devices that emit light through optical amplification.
Touchscreen Technology: Capacitive and resistive input interfaces.

Mining & Ore Processing

Rare Earth Element Extraction: Chemical processes to separate lanthanides required for electronics.

Polymers, Rubber, Plastics & Composites

Carbon Fiber Composites: Extremely strong and light fiber-reinforced plastics.
Silicones: Polysiloxanes used in sealants, adhesives, and medical applications.
Kevlar/Aramid Fibers: Heat-resistant and strong synthetic fibers.

Precision Manufacturing & Machine Tools

Photolithography: The process used in microfabrication to pattern parts of a thin film or the bulk of a substrate.
CNC Machining: Computer Numerical Control of machine tools.
Additive Manufacturing (3D Printing): Building objects layer-by-layer.
Clean Room Technology: Environments with low levels of environmental pollutants for manufacturing.
Surface Mount Technology (SMT): Component assembly directly onto PCBs.
MEMS (Micro-Electro-Mechanical Systems): Microscopic devices with moving parts.
Pick-and-Place Machines: Robotic assembly systems for electronics manufacturing.

Fuels & Energy Sources

Photovoltaic Cells (Solar Panels): Converting light directly into electricity.
Lithium-Ion Batteries: High energy density rechargeable electrochemical cells.
Wind Turbine Technology: Modern utility-scale renewable energy generation.
Fuel Cell Technology: Electrochemical energy conversion devices.

Power Transmission, Grids & Storage

Smart Grid: Electrical grids using digital technology to monitor and manage transport of electricity.
High-Voltage Direct Current (HVDC): Efficient transmission of power over long distances.

Urban Infrastructure & Public Works

Smart Cities Sensors: IoT devices for monitoring traffic, waste, and air quality.

Water Supply, Sanitation & Waste Systems

Desalination (Reverse Osmosis): Water purification using partially permeable membranes.

Transport & Vehicles (Land, Sea & Air)

Electric Vehicle Powertrains: Battery, inverter, and electric motor systems replacing ICE.
Unmanned Aerial Vehicles (Drones): Aircraft without a human pilot aboard.

Navigation, Orientation & Exploration

Global Positioning System (GPS/GNSS): Satellite-based radio navigation systems.
Inertial Guidance Systems: Navigation using motion sensors and gyroscopes.
LIDAR: Light Detection and Ranging for mapping and autonomous navigation.

Warfare, Weapons & Fortification

Stealth Technology: Low-observable technology to make personnel/vehicles less visible to radar/infrared.
Precision-Guided Munitions: Weapons that can direct themselves to a target.
Cyberwarfare Tools: Weaponized software for espionage or sabotage (e.g., Stuxnet).

Health, Medicine & Public Health

Computed Tomography (CT/CAT): X-ray measurements processed to produce tomographic images.
Monoclonal Antibodies: Lab-made molecules engineered to serve as substitute antibodies.
Telemedicine Platforms: Remote distribution of health services.
Pacemakers & Implantable Defibrillators: Devices regulating heart rhythm via electrical impulses.
mRNA Vaccines: Synthetic RNA used to induce immune response.
Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI): Non-invasive imaging using magnetic fields and radio waves.
Robotic Surgery Systems: Computer-assisted surgical platforms.
Electronic Health Records (EHR): Digital patient data management systems.